preserveWorkflowInstanceSchedule uses a scoped query, so it won’t find soft-deleted workflow_instances. Re-importing a soft-deleted instance would then recalculate NextScheduledAt from "now" and potentially clear LastExecutedAt, even though DeletedAt is intentionally preserved during the upsert. Use Unscoped() for the lookup so scheduling fields are preserved for soft-deleted rows too.
Replies:
**lisa implementation guidance:** In cmd/seed/workflows.go, update preserveWorkflowInstanceSchedule so the existing workflow instance lookup includes soft-deleted rows. Change the query from tx.Select("next_scheduled_at", "last_executed_at").First(&existing, "id = ?", instance.ID) to tx.Unscoped().Select("next_scheduled_at", "last_executed_at").First(&existing, "id = ?", instance.ID). Keep the rest of the function behavior unchanged: if a row is found, copy NextScheduledAt and LastExecutedAt from the existing row; only calculate a new schedule when no physical row exists and cadence is non-empty. Add or update a focused test in cmd/seed/workflows_test.go that creates a soft-deleted WorkflowInstance with existing NextScheduledAt and LastExecutedAt, calls preserveWorkflowInstanceSchedule or imports/upserts the same seeded instance path, and asserts those timestamps are preserved rather than recalculated or cleared.

